   "id": "spcpt_YdsW9Vp2FCRF6bJM",
   "name": "Trigonometric Identities",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Trigonometric Identities"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a trigonometric identity?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Trigonometric identities** are statements about **trigonometric functions** like $\\mathrm{sin}θ$, $\\mathrm{cos}θ$ and $\\mathrm{tan}θ$\n\n  - They are **true** for **all values** of the angle $θ$\n  - They can be used to help **simplify** trigonometric equations before solving them\n- Sometimes you may see identities written with the **symbol ** $\\equiv$  instead of an equals sign\n\n  - This means 'identical to' or 'equivalent to'"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What trigonometric identities do I need to know?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- You must know these **two trigonometric identities**:\n\n  - $\\mathrm{tan}θ=\\frac{\\mathrm{sin}θ}{\\mathrm{cos}θ}$\n  \n    - This is the identity for $\\mathrm{tan}θ$\n  - $\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ+\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ=1$\n  \n    - This is sometimes called the Pythagorean identity\n    - Note that the notation $\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ$ is the same thing as $(\\mathrm{sin}θ)^{2}$\n- The second identity is often used in one of its **rearranged forms**\n\n  - $\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ=1-\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ$\n  - $\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ=1-\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- When asked to show that one thing is equal or identical to another, look at what parts are 'missing'\n\n  - This can help you spot which identity to use"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Show that the equation $2\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ-\\mathrm{cos}θ=0$ can be written in the form  $a\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ+b\\mathrm{cos}θ+c=0$, where $a$, $b$ and $c$ are integers to be found with $a>0$.\n\n> *Note that in the 'target' form there is no *$\\mathrm{sin}θ$* or *$\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ$\n\n> *That means we want to use a substitution to get rid of the *$\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ$* in the original form*\n\n> *We can do this using the identity *$\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ+\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ=1$*, rearranged as  *$\\mathrm{sin}^{2}θ=1-\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ$\n\n`table attributes columnalign right center left columnspacing 0px end attributes row cell 2 sin squared theta minus cos theta end cell equals cell 2 open parentheses 1 space minus space cos squared theta close parentheses minus cos theta end cell row blank equals cell 2 minus 2 cos squared theta minus cos theta end cell row blank equals cell negative 2 cos squared theta minus cos theta plus 2 end cell end table`\n\n> *Substitute that back into the original equation*\n\n`table row cell negative 2 cos squared theta minus cos theta plus 2 end cell equals 0 end table`\n\n> *Multiply both sides of the equation by *$-1$* to make the *$\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ$* coefficient positive*\n\n> *This gets the equation into the required form with  *$a=2$*, *$b=1$* and *$c=-2$\n\n$2\\mathrm{cos}^{2}θ+\\mathrm{cos}θ-2=0$"
  }
 ]
}